+All computations are exact, over $\mathbb{Z}[t^{\pm1}]$ or $\mathbb{Q}$. We used $\sigma_i\mapsto I_{i-1}\oplus\begin{pmatrix}1-t&t\\1&0\end{pmatrix}\oplus I_{n-i-1}$, the matrices of \cite[Section 4.2]{BB05}, to which Section 2 of \cite{BBB} refers for Burau's original definition. By Fox calculus these are the matrices of the action on $H$ in the basis of lifts of $x_1,\dots,x_n$, with $t$ the deck transformation corresponding to each $x_k$, when $\sigma_i$ acts by $x_i\mapsto x_ix_{i+1}x_i^{-1}$, $x_{i+1}\mapsto x_i$. We also used the transposed matrices, the reduced representation in which $\sigma_i$ replaces row $i$ of $I_{n-1}$ by $(t,-t,1)$ in columns $i-1,i,i+1$, and its transpose; conjugating this transpose by $\mathrm{diag}(1,-1,1,\dots)$ gives the reduced matrices of \cite[Section 4.2]{BB05}. Replacing every $\sigma_i$ by the opposite half twist, or $t$ by $t^{-1}$, changes all of these by $t\mapsto t^{-1}$ up to conjugation. This does not affect statements at $t=-1$ or about depth, and for the other evaluations we also use $t=1/2$ and $t=1/3$. Triviality is checked with the Artin action of $\B_n$ on the free group $F_n$, and $f$ is $\sigma_i\mapsto\sigma_i$. In each of the four matrix conventions:
 \begin{itemize}
 \item $\rho_4(\Phi_0)\at=I$ and $\rho_5(f(\Phi_0))\at=I$;
-\item $\rho_4(\Phi_0)\neq I$ as a matrix over $\mathbb{Z}[t^{\pm1}]$, and $\rho_4(\Phi_0)|_{t=2}\neq I$, $\rho_4(\Phi_0)|_{t=3}\neq I$; the same holds for $\rho_5(f(\Phi_0))$;
+\item $\rho_4(\Phi_0)\neq I$ as a matrix over $\mathbb{Z}[t^{\pm1}]$, and $\rho_4(\Phi_0)|_{t=x}\neq I$ for $x=2,\tfrac12,3,\tfrac13$; the same holds for $\rho_5(f(\Phi_0))$;
 \item $\rho_4(\Phi_0)\not\equiv I\pmod{(t+1)^2}$, so the depth of $\Phi_0$ is exactly $1$;
-\item $\rho_4(X)-I=(1+t^3)\bigl(\rho_4(\Delta_3^2)-I\bigr)$.
+\item $\rho_4(X)-I=(1+t^3)\bigl(\rho_4(\Delta_3^2)-I\bigr)$, with $1+t^{-3}$ in place of $1+t^3$ when every $\sigma_i$ is replaced by $\sigma_i^{-1}$.
 \end{itemize}
-Moreover $\Phi_0$ acts nontrivially on $F_4$ (already $X$ and $b$ do not commute), deleting any one strand gives a braid whose word freely reduces to the empty word, $\Phi_0=[\delta^{-2},b]$ with $\delta=\Delta_3^{-2}\Delta_4^2=A_{14}A_{24}A_{34}$, and $\rho_4(\Phi_0)$ and $\rho_4(\sigma_3\Phi_0\sigma_3^{-1})$ do not commute. The attached script \texttt{phi0\_check.py} (Python 3, standard library only, about a second to run) performs all of these checks.
+Moreover $\Phi_0$ acts nontrivially on $F_4$ (already $X$ and $b$ do not commute), deleting any one strand gives a braid whose word freely reduces to the empty word, $\Phi_0=[\delta^{-2},b]$ with $\delta=\Delta_3^{-2}\Delta_4^2=A_{14}A_{24}A_{34}$ (in the free basis $A_{14},A_{24},A_{34}$ of $K_4$ this is a reduced word of $28$ letters with exponent sums zero), $\rho_4(\Phi_0)$ and $\rho_4(\sigma_3\Phi_0\sigma_3^{-1})$ do not commute, and $[\Phi_0,\sigma_3\Phi_0\sigma_3^{-1}]$ has depth exactly $2$. The script \texttt{phi0\_check.py} sent with this note (Python 3, standard library only, a few seconds to run) performs all of these checks, checks the values from Section 7 of \cite{BBB} quoted after Lemma~\ref{lem:sign}, and prints the freely reduced word for $\Phi_0$.
 
